.TH ZTOP 1 "1 November 2026" "amix-z3660rtg" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ztop \- show the Z3660's temperatures, voltages and CPU clock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ztop
[
.B \-1
] [
.B \-i
.I seconds
] [
.B \-d
.I device
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ztop
reads the measurements the Z3660 accelerator's firmware makes \(em the
values the AmigaOS ZTop shows \(em through the RTG driver, and shows them on
the terminal, refreshed every two seconds: the FPGA, board and 68060
temperatures, the FPGA core and auxiliary voltages, the 3.3 V and 5 V rails,
the supply of the LTC2990 monitor chip, and the CPU clock the preset sets.
For each measurement it shows the latest value, a running average (each new
value weighs one tenth, as in ZTop), and the lowest and highest seen.
On a terminal the screen is redrawn; otherwise each refresh is printed as a
block followed by an empty line.
.PP
It reads the information node
.IR /dev/zz9000i ,
which any user may open, and falls back to
.IR /dev/zz9000 .
It writes nothing to the board.
A measurement the firmware does not offer is left out.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-1
Print each measurement once, one line each, and exit.
A line is a name, the value and its unit:
.BR fpga_temp ,
.BR board_temp ,
.B cpu_temp
in degrees Celsius,
.BR vcore ,
.BR vaux ,
.BR v3v3 ,
.BR v5 ,
.B vcc
in volts and
.B cpu_mhz
in MHz, for example
.RS
.nf
cpu_temp 45.6 C
v5 5.02 V
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI \-i " seconds"
Refresh every
.I seconds
(1 to 60; the default is 2).
.TP
.BI \-d " device"
Read this device instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the values were shown; 1 when the device cannot be opened or its
driver has no sensors call; 2 for a usage error; 3 when the board offers no
sensors (a ZZ9000, or Z3660 firmware without them).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/zz9000i
the RTG driver's information node (character 49, minor 128, mode 0444)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xztop (1)
.SH NOTES
The temperatures and voltages are the firmware's latest measurement; it
takes a new set about once a second.
The CPU clock is the value the preset asks for, not a measurement.
